---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GuideNav from './_GuideNav.astro';
import { guides } from './_guides.ts';

const guide = guides.find(item => item.path === 'make-it-yours/')!;
const config = `export const siteConfig: SiteConfig = {
  name: 'Your project',
  author: { name: 'Your name', url: 'https://example.com/' },
  repository: 'https://github.com/you/your-project',
  socialImages: true,
};`;
const page = `---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="Who runs this project and why.">
  <main>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>Write your page content here.</p>
  </main>
</Layout>`;
---

<Layout title={guide.title} description={guide.description}>
  <main>
    <h1>{guide.title}</h1>
    <p>{guide.description}</p>
    <p>
      A copy of the starter still describes the starter: its name, its author,
      its icons and these guides. The steps below replace each of those with
      your own, in roughly the order you will want to do them. None of them needs
      a new dependency.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="copy">
      <h2 id="copy">Get a copy</h2>
      <p>
        On GitHub, choose <q>Use this template</q> to create your own repository,
        then clone it. Install with <code>pnpm install --frozen-lockfile</code> and
        start the development server with <code>pnpm dev</code>. You need Node.js
        24.8.0 or newer; pnpm's version is pinned in <code>package.json</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="url">
      <h2 id="url">Set your production URL</h2>
      <p>
        Uncomment <code>site</code> in <code>astro.config.mjs</code> and set it to
        your origin, such as <code>https://example.com</code>, or pass it with
        <code>--site</code> when you build. The starter leaves it unset so a fork
        never publishes canonical URLs for someone else's domain, and the build
        fails until you supply one. Add <code>base</code> only if the site lives
        under a subpath.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="identity">
      <h2 id="identity">Name the site and its author</h2>
      <p>
        <code>src/config.ts</code> holds everything that identifies the site:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{config}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>name</code> ends every inner page title and starts the homepage
        title. <code>author</code> is credited in the footer and named as the
        publisher in the homepage's structured data; <code>repository</code> is
        linked from the footer. Delete either line to drop what it drives; with
        neither, the footer disappears. <code>socialImages</code> switches the
        generated title cards on or off.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="pages">
      <h2 id="pages">Write your pages</h2>
      <p>
        Rewrite <code>src/pages/index.astro</code>; keep <code>home</code> on its
        layout and change its <code>title</code> to a short line on what your site
        does. Add routes as files in <code>src/pages/</code>, each with its own
        title and description:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{page}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Add styles and scripts the usual Astro way. The output checks allow them;
        the starter simply ships none.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="icons">
      <h2 id="icons">Replace the icons</h2>
      <p>
        The starter ships a neutral C mark. Generate your own set with
        <a href="https://favseed.cevdet.ch">favseed</a>: give it one SVG and it
        returns <code>icon.svg</code>, <code>favicon.ico</code>,
        <code>apple-touch-icon.png</code>, <code>icon-192.png</code> and
        <code>icon-512.png</code>, all in the browser, with no upload. Copy them
        into <code>public/</code> over the files with the same names, and replace
        <code>logo.svg</code> with your own logo. The layout declares only
        <code>favicon.ico</code>; the README shows how to add the Apple icon and a
        web app manifest if you need them. Browsers cache icons hard, so check
        in a private window after you deploy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cards">
      <h2 id="cards">Change or drop the title cards</h2>
      <p>
        Edit <code>src/lib/social-card.ts</code> to change the card artwork, and
        keep the font's licence if you swap the font. To stop generating cards,
        set <code>socialImages: false</code>; manual images keep working. The
        README lists the files and the package to delete if you want the feature
        gone entirely.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="remove-docs">
      <h2 id="remove-docs">Remove these guides</h2>
      <p>
        These guides describe the starter, not your project. When you no longer
        need them, delete <code>src/pages/docs/</code> and the documentation
        section of <code>src/pages/index.astro</code>. Nothing else refers to
        them: the sitemap is built from the pages that exist, so it drops them on
        the next build.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="check">
      <h2 id="check">Check and deploy</h2>
      <p>
        Run <code>pnpm verify</code> with your <code>site</code> configured. It
        type-checks, runs both test suites, builds and audits the output. Then
        follow the <a href="../cloudflare-pages/">Cloudflare Pages guide</a>, or
        deploy the <code>dist/</code> directory to any static host that serves
        <code>404.html</code> with a 404 status. Keep the MIT licence notice that
        covers the starter's code.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideNav current={guide.path} />
  </main>
</Layout>
